  "text_md": "a cell-impermeant, non uorescent, high-a nity DNA stain that forms highly  uorescent and stable dye–nucleic acid complexes when bound to DNA.  us, staining of nuclear DNA by the Image-iT® DEAD Green™ viability stain cannot occur in live cells due to the impermeability of the plasma membrane to the stain. Drugs and test compounds that lead to serious cell injuries, including plasma membrane permeability, allow entry of the Image-iT® DEAD Green™ viability stain, enabling discrimination of dead cells. Hoechst 33342, which stains nuclear DNA in live and dead cells, is included in the kit as a segmentation tool for automated image analysis.\n\n e HCS DNA Damage Kit contains su cient material to perform the DNA damage assay on two 96-well plates when used as described in the protocol provided:\n\nImage-iT® Dead Green viability stain pH2AX mouse monoclonal antibody\n\npHZAX mouse monoclonal antibody\n\nAlexa Fluor 555 goat anti-mouse IgG antibody\n\nHoechst 33342 nucleic acid stain\n\nDetailed protocols\n\nBecause no single parameter fully de nes cell viability in all systems, it is o en useful to use multiple approaches to study cytotoxicity. In addition to the HCS DNA Damage Kit, we o er the HCS LIVE⁄DEAD® Green Kit (H10290) and the HCS Mitochondrial Health Kit (H10295), both of which employ the Image-iT® DEAD™ Green viability stain. Additionally, the HCS LIVE/DEAD® Kit provides a choice of two nucleic acid stains—the far-red– uorescent HCS NuclearMask™ Deep Red stain (H10294, Section 12.5) and the blue- uorescent Hoechst 33342 (H1399, H3570, H21492; Section 12.5)—for use as cell-permeant nuclear segmentation tools.  e HCS Mitochondrial Health Kit provides Image-iT® DEAD Green viability stain and Hoechst 33342 stain,\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n(CH 2 ) 3  O 3\n\nHO\n\n(CH 3 CH 2 ) 2 N\n\nFigure 15.5.11 F2N12S, a component of the Violet Ratio metric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit (A35137).\n\nas well as the MitoHealth stain, which accumulates in the mitochondria of live cells in proportion to the mitochondrial membrane potential. Both of these kits provide su cient reagents to perform the assays on two 96-well plates, using the protocol provided.\n\n# Apoptosis Assays that Detect Membrane Asymmetry\n\n# Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit  e Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry\n\nProbe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit (A35137) provides a simple and fast method for detecting apoptosis with dead-cell discrimination by  ow cytometry.  e violet ratiometric membrane asymmetry probe, F2N12S (4 N , N diethylamino-6-( N -dodecylN -methylN -(3-sulfopropyl))ammoniomethyl-3-hydroxy avone, Figure 15.5.11), is a novel violet diode–excitable dye for the detection of membrane phospholipid asymmetry changes during apoptosis.  is dye exhibits an excited-state intramolecular proton transfer (ESIPT) reaction, resulting in a dual  uorescence with two emission bands corresponding to 530 nm and 585 nm and producing a two-color ratiometric response to variations in surface charge. 59  is ratiometric probe is therefore a self-calibrating indicator of apoptotic transformation, which is independent of probe concentration, cell size and instrument variation, such as  uctuations of laser intensity or sensitivity of the detectors. Given that apoptosis modi es the surface charge of the outer lea et\n\nof the plasma membrane, F2N12S can be used to monitor changes in membrane asymmetry that occur during apoptosis through a change in the relative intensity of the two emission bands of the dye 59 (Figure 15.5.12).  e F2N12S-based apoptosis assay allows samples to be analyzed a er a 5-minute incubation at room temperature and does not require special bu ers or wash steps.  is kit can be paired with other reagents such as MitoProbe™ DiIC 1 (5) or annexin V for multiparametric analysis of apoptosis and viability.  e kit components, number of assays and assay principles are summarized in Table 15.4.\n\n# Annexin V Conjugates  e human vascular\n\nanticoagulant annexin V is a 35–36 kilodalton, Ca 2+ -dependent phospholipid-binding protein that has a high af nity for the anionic phospholipid phosphatidylserine (PS). In normal\n\n262,144\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n1\n\n196,608\n\nA+D\n\n131,072\n\nẵ\n\n65,536\n\n65,536\n\n131,072\n\n196,608\n\n262,144\n\nF2N12S orange  uorescence\n\n262,144\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nB\n\n196,608\n\nA+D\n\n131,072\n\n65,536\n\n65,536\n\n131,072\n\n196,608\n\n262,144\n\nF2N12S orange  uorescence\n\n1\n\n1\n\n10 5\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\n10 4\n\n10 2\n\n1,250\n\n2,500\n\n3,750\n\n5,000\n\nF2N12S ratio 585 nm/530 nm  uorescence x 1,000\n\n1,000\n\nfluorescence x\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n10 4\n\n10 3\n\n10 2\n\n10'\n\n1,250\n\n2,500\n\n3,750\n\nF2N12S ratio 585 nm/530 nm  uorescence x 1,000\n\nfluorescence x 1,000\n\nFigure 15.5.12 Jurkat cells (T-cell leukemia, human) treated with 10  M camptothecin for 4 hours ( B and D ) or untreated ( A and C ), control. Cells were stained according to the protocol. Samples were analyzed on a  ow cytometer with 405 nm excitation using 585 nm and 530 nm bandpass  lters for F2N12S and 488 nm excitation for SYTOX® AADvanced™ dead cell stain using a 695 nm bandpass  lter. In panels A and B, live cells can be discriminated from apoptotic and dead cells by the relative intensities of the two emission bands from F2N12S. In C and D, SYTOX® AADvanced™ dead cell stain  uorescence is plotted against a derived ratio parameter from the two emission bands (585/530 nm) of F2N12S. A = apoptotic cells, L = live cells, D = dead cells.\n\ninvitrogen\n\n![](<01ed6dcc6a3d0237daa88b293d0c0667df1a_page_67_pg1_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\nby Thermo Fisher Scientific\n\n",
